# ─── EngineContent SFTP endpoint, local build only ────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to cmake/EngineContentCredentials.cmake (WITHOUT .template) and
# fill in the values. That name is gitignored: this repository is public, so the
# endpoint never goes into a tracked file. CI builds get the same values from
# repository secrets — see .github/workflows/ci.yml.
#
# src/HE_ContentSync/CMakeLists.txt includes the copy when it exists, and what it
# sets here wins over everything else on this machine. Having no file at all is a
# normal state: the Editor then reports the remote library as "not configured"
# and behaves exactly as it did before the feature existed.
#
# Plain set(), deliberately — NOT set(... CACHE ...). A cache set without FORCE
# does nothing once the entry already exists, so in a build directory you had
# configured before, a CACHE version of this file would be silently ignored.
# (The CMakeLists declares those cache entries BEFORE including this file, which
# is what lets these plain set()s win — see the comment there for why the other
# order quietly dropped them on a fresh build directory.)
#
# After copying this file: configure once. The build system re-checks for this
# file's arrival on its own (file(GLOB … CONFIGURE_DEPENDS)), and the configure
# output then names the endpoint — "HorizonContentSync: EngineContent endpoint
# <user>@<host>:22". If it still says "not configured", nothing here took.
#
# Rotation: change the password here AND in the ENGINE_CONTENT_PASSWORD secret.
set(HE_ENGINE_CONTENT_HOST     "")   # e.g. "access-xxxxxxx.webspace-host.com"
set(HE_ENGINE_CONTENT_PORT     "22")
set(HE_ENGINE_CONTENT_USER     "")
set(HE_ENGINE_CONTENT_PASSWORD "")
set(HE_ENGINE_CONTENT_BASEPATH "")   # "" = the account's own root
